  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 335 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Unsettled weather and above-normal rain chances are \nexpected late this week, with isolated heavy downpours possible.\n\nOur current setup featuring surface high pressure nosing into NC \nfrom the N within below-normal PW will undergo a transition to a \nwetter pattern with increasing rain chances and higher humidity by \nThu. The surface high will push E off the Mid Atlantic coast this \nmorning, allowing a diffuse stalled surface frontal zone stretching \nfrom IA/MO SE and E through AL/GA/SC to off the S NC coast to lift \nback northward through NC today. The ensuing increasing low level \nsoutherly-component flow will draw higher dewpoints into central NC, \nwhile aloft, above-normal PW now over the central Plains and mid \nMiss Valley will swing SE and E into the Carolinas. Our mid level \nflow will be gently cyclonic as a baggy trough holds just to our W, \nreinforced by digging shortwave energy through S Ont/N MI, and \nnestled between two strong ridges over the Atlantic and the Desert \nSouthwest. As periodic upper level speed maxes rotate through the \ntrough base to the E side of the trough, the bouts of RRQ upper \ndivergence, modest DPVA, above-normal PW, increasing surface \ndewpoints, and moderate SBCAPE will all contribute to increased rain \nchances for central NC, particularly late Thu through much of Sat. \nGiven expected PWs over 2\", deep warm cloud layer over 4 km favoring \nwarm rain processes, and potential somewhat slow storm motion and \ntraining, pockets of heavy downpours and high rain totals will be \npossible. Limited coverage precludes consideration of a flood watch, \nbut at least a few flood advisories may be needed. Rain chances will \nhead back down late in the weekend as the upper level shear axis \npivots clockwise to a W-E orientation and pushes to our S, allowing \nmid-upper ridging to build from the mid Miss Valley through the Mid \nAtlantic. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above-normal temperatures likely to end August and \npersist into early September.\n\nThe persistent anomalous mid/upper lvl ridge and anticyclone over \nthe Four Corner region will be displaced by a series of shortwave \ntroughs progress through the PacNW and northern/central \nIntermountain West. This process will gradually build mid/upper lvl \nheights over the Deep South and TN Valley early next week and likely \npersisting into early September. \n\nThis height pattern will be favorable for positive H850 temp \nanomalies to spread into the region and favor well-above normal \ntemperatures. Although the temperature forecast will naturally be \ncomplicated by late morning and afternoon convective chances, deep \nlayer of anomalous lower/mid tropospheric temps may help mitigate \nthe widespread convective chances. As a result, temperatures in the \nmid/upper 90s will be possible Tues onward with the experimental \nHeatRisk having a 40-70% chance of reaching the Major risk category.",
